Description: stripped-down Firefox kiosk that takes the screen at boot
 Bootowser replaces the boot splash with a real web browser. It runs a
 patched Firefox 156 build -- no tabs, no address bar, no menus, no
 developer tools, no remote experiments -- under a managed policy that
 locks it down, and it starts automatically at boot showing one
 fullscreen page.
 .
 It is aimed at single-purpose devices: signage, information kiosks,
 menu boards and TV-style displays where a full desktop environment is more
 than the device needs.
 .
 The browser is a separately built, patched Firefox tree (see the firefox/
 directory in the upstream repository). This package ships the runtime, the
 systemd units and a minimal X server configuration. It ships no boot splash
 of its own: whatever Plymouth theme the system already uses keeps showing
 during boot, and Bootowser waits for plymouth-quit-wait.service before
 taking over the screen.
 .
 A control sidecar lets the kiosk page request shell commands over a
 loopback-only HTTP API, with root hooks limited to the shipped scripts in
 /usr/lib/bootowser/commands via sudo.